f they're holding singles or broken pairs.\n\nThe key is teamwork.  As a Peasant, especially with a teammate actively fighting, I shouldn't just beat my teammate's hand unless there is a strategic block (usually singles) I need to put in place. So, if I pass, my teammate might get a free play, and that's good! My hand isn't in a winning position, I am better off saving the aces, and I will be working with my teammate, giving him a chance to work on the game.\n\nUltimately, I'm going to pass. It's the strategically sound move. I'll conserve my Aces for a more critical moment, and I'll trust my teammate to continue the fight.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate (Previous Player) played the pair of 9s to beat the Landlord\'s pair of 8s. As a peasant, I should generally avoid beating my teammate\'s cards unless I can play out my hand immediately or if I need to forcefully take control to prevent the Landlord from passing weak cards. My hand (66, J, K, A, A, 2, BJ) is strong in high cards but not ready to go out immediately due to the small pair and unconnected singles. Playing the Aces now would waste a powerful control hand on my partner. It is better to let the Landlord try to beat the 9s. If the Landlord plays a higher pair (e.g., QQs or KKs), I can then use my Aces to capture the lead. If the Landlord passes, my teammate maintains control, which is the goal.\n\nReason: Beating a teammate\'s pair of 9s with a pair of Aces is strategically poor here. It wastes a top-tier stopper that should be reserved for the Landlord, and it interrupts the teammate\'s flow. Passing gives the teammate a chance to continue or forces the Landlord to spend high cards.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
